# What must never enter the build context, and why each line is here.
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` are both required, and the second is the one that is easy to forget: an
# operator who copies `.env.example` to `.env.production` on their laptop has just made a file the
# first pattern does not match, and `COPY . .` would bake it into a layer of an image published to
# a registry. In this service those files hold the node RPC URLs, which carry HTTP Basic userinfo.
#
# `.git` is here for the same reason one step removed: the history holds every version of every
# file, so shipping it undoes any secret that was ever committed and then deleted.
#
# The org's secret-hygiene workflow greps this file for `.env`, `.env.*` and `.git` as exact lines.
# That is not a style check — it is the only thing standing between a careless `COPY . .` and a
# credential in a published layer.
.env
.env.*
.git
.github

# Build output and installed trees. node_modules is reinstalled inside the image against the
# image's own platform rather than copied from whatever the developer's machine resolved.
node_modules
dist
*.tsbuildinfo
*.log
.DS_Store

# Documentation. It changes far more often than the code and would invalidate the layer cache on
# every edit for no benefit — nothing at runtime reads it.
README.md
